      "id": 1,
      "text": "Carter Springs grew from a Washoe homeland into a pioneer settlement when early settlers tapped the valley’s abundant springs for ranching.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        1,
        6
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://grokipedia.com/page/carter_springs_nevada",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "In 1851, Mormon pioneers founded nearby Genoa, a trading post that served emigrants on the California Trail.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        2
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://grokipedia.com/page/carter_springs_nevada",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "The Carter family arrived around 1864, operating a station and supply store that became a hub for miners and ranchers.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        3
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://grokipedia.com/page/carter_springs_nevada",
      "category": "people",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "Carter Springs sits at 5,522 feet, where alluvial fans and floodplains from the Carson River shape open meadows.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        25
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://grokipedia.com/page/carter_springs_nevada",
      "category": "nature",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "The region’s geology reflects the Basin and Range extension, with the Carson Valley carved between the Sierra Nevada’s Carson Range and the Pine Nut Mountains.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        26
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://grokipedia.com/page/carter_springs_nevada",
      "category": "nature",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 6,
      "text": "After the post‑Civil War boom, Carter Springs transitioned from a mining support spot to a stable ranching community, preserving its pioneer heritage.",
